00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.927
So just to give you some context on
the why of choosing this system,

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.593
having an n8n system running on
the cloud on your own server

00:00:47.753 --> 00:00:51.740
allows you to have it first always on as
if you were to fork it on your computer,

00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:56.353
you don't pay per-run pricing and overall
this cuts about half of the costs when it

00:00:56.413 --> 00:01:01.360
comes to the cloud costs of running
these models on n8n's own cloud.

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:05.567
You have access to the server logs which
is something that you don't get via the

00:01:05.687 --> 00:01:09.707
cloud which allows you to find problems
that you would not find otherwise.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.160
And of course your keys and your credentials
are more protected because they live

00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:15.307
within an instance that you fully control.

00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:19.220
It's quite interesting actually because
via the server logs you can query the

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.760
database directly. So you can basically
live code troubleshooting in a way that is

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.113
extremely dynamic and fast, which you
can't do once you're using a normal cloud

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:32.627
subscription. So this is just a system
in numbers, so you get a glimpse of it.

00:01:32.873 --> 00:01:37.560
It has around 1000 workflows running per
week, and there's a few caps that I found

00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:40.573
necessary due to constraints
while running the model.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.640
And this is the architecture of
the model in very simple terms.

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:46.800
So the model is connected to my
email inbox and to some job boards.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.073
I actually should give you a bit of context
on what these specific workflows are

00:01:51.227 --> 00:01:54.987
doing so that you understand in which type
of context you can apply this yourself.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.460
So for my specific case, I tested these
on different types of things, but this

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.467
specific one is a job finder.

00:02:01.627 --> 00:02:06.393
So I give it very specific criteria, and
there's several workflows that run in

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:11.493
tandem that basically populates a sheet for
me with different open roles, then track

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:16.300
my results, and also based on different
fields that I set up in the sheets, for

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.527
example, whether some follow-up emails can
or not be sent, it will send those emails

00:02:21.587 --> 00:02:24.087
for me and also draft a
whole follow-up strategy.

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:28.353
So different workflows are responsible for
different parts of this complex system,

00:02:28.553 --> 00:02:32.700
and in very simple terms, this is the
architecture, so it reads my email inbox and

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.040
job boards once every week in order to
extract as many roles as possible and to

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.867
understand how I'm doing in each process.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.480
And it afterwards does
three different actions.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.320
So in order to save tokens, it skips
things in order to solve, like in terms of

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.700
emails. So there's a memory.

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:49.073
I'll talk about that later.

